 ## Overview
-SPH Attribute Entropy for Importance Sampling is a precomputation technique that assigns sampling probabilities to SPH particles based on the local information complexity of their attributes and the presence of phase interfaces [S1]. It solves the problem of high-frequency shading errors and subsampling artifacts at phase boundaries by ensuring that regions with high attribute variation or discontinuous phase transitions are sampled more densely [S1]. It differs from naive random discarding by using a structured, data-driven importance grid that accounts for the spatial support of the SPH kernel, and it differs from its sibling [Stochastic Volume Rendering of Multi-Phase SPH Data](3638-stochastic-volume-rendering-of-multi-phase-sph-data.md) by explicitly computing entropy to quantify attribute variation rather than relying solely on stochastic selection heuristics.
+SPH Attribute Entropy for Importance Sampling is a precomputation technique that assigns sampling probabilities to SPH particles based on the local information complexity of their attributes and the presence of phase interfaces [S1]. It solves the problem of high-frequency shading errors and subsampling artifacts at phase boundaries by ensuring that regions with high attribute variation or discontinuous phase transitions are sampled more densely [S1]. It differs from naive random discarding by using a structured, data-driven importance grid that accounts for the spatial support of the SPH kernel, and it differs from its sibling [Stochastic Volume Rendering of Multi-Phase SPH Data](3638-stochastic-volume-rendering-of-multi-phase-sph-data.md) by explicitly computing entropy to quantify attribute variation rather than relying solely on stochastic selection heuristics [G].
 
